11  a266p.;c21cm.  aIncludes index.  aStrategic planning.00682nam a2200229 a 4500001000600000005001000006020001800016040001100034041000800045082001400053090001600067100002700083245007100110246004900181260005700230300002400287500005300311650002800364650002200392650002500414650001300439533127/12/2020  a9781422193402  aDHSPKT  aEng  a158.1223  a158.1bH169  aHalvorson, Heidi Grant  a9 things successful people do differently/cHeidi Grant Halvorson.  aNine things successful people do differently  aBoston, Mass.:bHarvard Business Review Press,c2012  avi, 106p. ;c16 cm.  aIncludes bibliographical references (p. 93-101).  aAchievement motivation.  aGoal (Psychology)  aSuccess in business.  aSuccess.00493nam a2200169 a 4500001000600000005000900006020001800015040001100033041000800044082001500052090001700067245009100084260006500175300002200240650002800262650003300290496185/9/2019  a9781633690783  aDHSPKT  aEng  223a658.45  a658.45bD569  aDifficult conversations:bcraft a clear message, manage emotions, focus on a solution.  aBoston, Massachusetts:bHarvard Business Review Press,c2016  aix, 110p.;c18cm.  aBusiness communication.  aInterpersonal communication.01642nam a2200229 a 4500001000600000005001000006020001800016040001100034041000800045082001700053090001900070245008100089260006500170300002400235490003000259500005100289520095200340650002801292650001901320650002401339710004901363533107/12/2020  a9781633691476  aDHSPKT  aEng  a658.4022223  a658.4022bV819  aVirtual collaboration:bwork from anywhere, overcommunicate, avoid isolation  aBoston, Massachusetts:bHarvard Business Review Press,c2016  axii, 121p. ;c18 cm  a20 minute manager series.  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  aWhether you work at a home office or in one location of a global company, you need to work well with others to meet your professional and organizational goals. This book offers strategies for collaborating with colleagues more effectively no matter where you are. You'll learn tips and strategies for addressing a wide range of virtual work challenges, from conference calls to team chat room etiquette to navigating time zones to coping when technologies fail to avoiding feeling disconnected when you're not interacting one-on-one at the coffee station. Virtual Collaboration guides you through the basics of: - Deciding whether remote work is right for you - Communicating effectively over a variety of media - Clarifying the processes, goals, and tools you'll use to communicate and collaborate with your colleagues - Navigating common challenges, such as technology glitches, isolation, conflict, and performance issuescProvided by publisher  aBusiness communication.  aTelecommuting.  aVirtual work teams.  aHarvard Business Review Press,eissuing body00594nam a2200193 a 4500001000600000005000900006020001800015040001100033041000800044082001800052090001900070245009400089260006500183300002300248500002000271650003300291650004300324650003300367496155/9/2019  a9781625275424  aDHSPKT  aEng  223a658.312 5  a658.3125bG539  aGiving effective feedback:bcheck in regularly, handle convversations, bring out the best  aBoston, Massachusetts:bHarvard Business Review Press,c2014  aviii, 96p.;c18cm.  aIncludes index.  aCommunication in management.  aCommunication in personnel management.  aInterpersonal communication.00582nam a2200193 a 4500001000600000005000900006020001800015040001100033041000800044082001500052090001700067245009100084260006400175300002300239500006600262650002400328650001600352650002000368444606/3/2018  a9781625275431  aDHSPKT  aEng  223a650.11  a650.11bG394  aGetting work done:bPrioritize your work, be more efficient, take control of your time  aBoston, Masachusetts:bHarvard Business Review Press,c2014  avii, 108p.;c18cm.  aIncludes bibliographical references (pages 91-101) and index.  aLabour productivity  aPerformance  aTime management00662nam a2200217 a 4500001000600000005001000006020001800016040001100034041000800045082001700053090001900070100001900089245009500108260006500203300002400268500006700292650002300359650001600382650001600398650003000414533177/12/2020  a9781633696969  aDHSPKT  aEng  223a658.4092  a658.4092bH645  aHill, Linda A.  aBecoming a manager:bhow new managers master the challenges of leadership/cLinda A. Hill.  aBoston, Massachusetts:bHarvard Business School Press,c2019  axix, 420p. ;c24 cm  aIncludes bibliographical references (pages 357-410) and index.  aExecutive ability.  aLeadership.  aManagement.  aSupervision of employees.00748nam a2200241 a 45000010006000000050011000060200018000170400011000350410008000460820017000540900019000711000018000902450126001082500031002342600051002653000022003165000066003386500020004046500026004246500025004507000015004757000016004904676719/11/2018  a9781422144114  aDHSPKT  aEng  a658.1511223  a658.1511bB516  aBerman, Karen  aFinancial intelligence:bA manager's guide to knowing what the numbers really mean/cKaren Berman, Joe Knight, John Case.  a2nd ed., rev. and expanded  aBoston :bHarvard business review press,c2013  axv, 284p. ;c24cm  aIncludes bibliographical references (p. [265]-266) and index.  aCash management  aCorporationsvFinance  aFinancial statements  aCase, John  aKnight, Joe00689nam a2200229 a 4500001000600000005000900006020001800015040001100033041000800044082001400052090001600066100002900082245004700111260005700158300002100215490003700236500006000273500004800333650001900381650002300400650003600423444546/3/2018  a9781422123126  aDHSPKT  aEng  a650.1223  a650.1bD794  aDrucker, Peter Ferdinand  aManaging oneself/cPeter Ferdinand Drucker  aBoston, Mass.:bHarvard Business School Press,c2008  av, 60 p.;c17 cm  aHarvard Business review classics  aReprint of an article from the Harvard business review.  aReprinted earlier in 1999 as Reprint 99204.  aCareer changes  aCareer development  aSelf-actualization (Psychology)01543nam a2200217 a 4500001000600000005001000006020001800016040001100034082001600045090001800061100002800079245006500107260006500172300001800237500006700255520091100322650002701233650003301260650001601293650001601309533167/12/2020  a9781633692350  aDHSPKT  a153.852223  a153.852bH169  aHalvorson, Heidi Grant.  aReinforcements:bhow to get people to help you/cHeidi Grant  aBoston, Massachusetts:bHarvard Business Review Press,c2018  a200p.;c22 cm  aIncludes bibliographical references (pages 179-187) and index.  aWe all need help--especially in today's uber-collaborative workplaces. Here's the good news: humans are naturally wired to want to help each other. Now here's the bad: asking for help makes most of us wildly uncomfortable. As a result, we do a poor job of calling in the reinforcements we need, leaving confused or even offended colleagues in our wake. This pragmatic book explains the research on what psychologists call social intelligence. To elicit helpful behavior from their colleagues, you need to do two things: 1) Remove the obstacles that stand in the way of them helping you; 2) Trigger one or more of the motivations that make people want to help. Whether you're a first-time manager or a seasoned leader, getting people to do things for you is what management is. This book will help you do so, and do it in a way that leaves your helpers feeling good about pitching in.cProvided by publisher  aHelp-seeking behavior.  aInterpersonal communication.  aManagement.  aPersuasion.01750nam a2200265 a 4500001000600000005001000006020001800016040001100034041000800045082001700053090001900070100002900089245010000118260006500218300001800283500005100301520096800352650001601320650001601336650003901352650000801391650003801399650002501437650002201462533157/12/2020  a9781633696327  aDHSPKT  aEng  a306.3615223  a306.3615bC448  aChamorro-Premuzic, Tomas  aWhy do so many incompetent men become leaders?:b(and how to fix it)/cTomas Chamorro-Premuzic.  aBoston, Massachusetts:bHarvard Business Review Press,c2019  a218p.;c21cm.  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  aIn this provocative book, author Tomas Chamorro-Premuzic connects the dots and asks a powerful question: what if the reason for the lack of women at the top--and the presence of so many incompetent leaders who also happen to be men--is not that there are too many obstacles slowing women's advancement, but that there aren't enough career-testing obstacles for men? Marshalling decades of rigorous research on leadership to build his case, Chamorro-Premuzic points out that although women make up a minority of leaders, female leaders are often rated by both bosses and subordinates as more competent than their male peers. At the same time, most organizations continue to equate leadership potential with a handful of personality traits, like overconfidence and narcissism, that don't correlate with success. In other words, these traits may help people get nominated to leadership roles, but they backfire once the individual has the job.cProvided by publisher  aExecutives.  aLeadership.  aMale domination (Social structure)  aMen  aSex role in the work environment.  aSuccess in business.  aWomen executives.00598nam a2200205 a 4500001000600000005001000006020001800016040001100034041000800045082001700053090001900070100002100089245006100110260006500171300002300236500006700259650002300326650001600349650002700365533147/12/2020  a9781422184127  aDHSPKT  aEng  223a658.4092  a658.4092bI123  aIbarra, Herminia  aAct like a leader, think like a leader/cHerminia Ibarra  aBoston, Massachusetts:bHarvard Business Review Press,c2015  a221 pages;c24 cm.  aIncludes bibliographical references (pages 191-205) and index.  aExecutive ability.  aLeadership.  aOrganizational change.01215nam a2200205 a 4500001000600000005001000006020001800016040001100034041000800045082001500053090001700068245011200085260006500197300002800262500002000290520062700310650003000937650002000967650002200987533137/12/2020  a9781633693685  aDHSPKT  aEng  a658.11223  a658.11bH339  aHarvard Business Review entrepreneur's handbook:beverything you need to launch and grow your new business.  aBoston, Massachusetts:bHarvard Business Review Press,c2018  avi, 292p.:bill ; 24cm.  aIncludes index.  aStarting an independent new business is rife with both opportunity and risk. From the basics of writing a business plan to the challenges of financing your new venture, the HBR Entrepreneur's Handbook is your essential resource for getting your startup off the ground. Topics include: Navigating the world of venture capital funding Turning innovations into successful realities in the marketplace Building a business model that makes sense for your business Developing the sales and marketing programs needed for your venture What entrepreneurial leaders must do to build culture and set direction.cProvided by publisher  aNew business enterprises.  aStart-up costs.  Business planning.00467nam a2200193 a 4500001000600000005001000006020001800016040001100034041000800045082001600053090001800069100002800087245005300115260003000168300002300198650002200221650001100243650001900254533227/12/2020  a9780762725809  aDHSPKT  aEng  223a917.804  a917.804bH938  aHunsaker, Joyce Badgley  aThey call me Sacagawea/cJoyce Badgley Hunsaker.  aGuilford:bTwo Dot,c2003  a47p.:bill;c26cm.  aNative traditions  aTravel  aUnited States.00657nam a2200205 a 4500001000600000005001000006020001800016040001100034041000800045082001600053090001700069100001500086245009300101260006500194300002200259500005100281650001800332650005000350650005100400533247/12/2020  a9781633693296  aDHSPKT  aEng  a658.872